\DOC LOCAL_JDOUBLE_SAFETY_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_JDOUBLE_SAFETY_TAC : string -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Applies a named universally quantified subroutine-correctness assumption to
discharge a point-doubling call inside an {eventually} goal, advancing the
symbolic state from one step index to the next and replacing the old state's
assumptions by ones describing the state after the call.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument, {assump_name : string}, is the label of the assumption in
the goal's assumption list that gets instantiated and used for the call; the
second, {n : int}, is the index used to name the new symbolic state variable
introduced for the post-call state. The goal's conclusion must be an
{eventually} statement about a state variable; the resulting conclusion is the
same {eventually} statement about a freshly named successor state variable. All
assumptions mentioning the old state variable — register/flag/memory reads, the
loaded-bytes fact, the event-trace equation and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
relation — are dropped and replaced by corresponding assumptions about the new
state, with the {MAYCHANGE} relation extended by the frame of the called
routine and the event trace rewritten as an {APPEND} of a fresh existential
trace variable {x1} with the previous trace. Two further assumptions about {x1}
are added: one equating it to a trace-generating function applied to the call's
arguments, and one asserting that its memory accesses are in bounds. The named
assumption itself is kept, but moved to the end of the assumption list; the
other quantified correctness assumption keeps its label.
{
       A ?- t
     ======================================  LOCAL_JDOUBLE_SAFETY_TAC assump_name n
       A' ?- t'
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, this single-subgoal transformation is applied to goals
of this one shape.

\ENDDOC
